.TH TEA 1 "2026-08-15" "tea 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
tea, sh-tea \- transparent pipeline stage logger (tee pun)
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B FILTER
.RI [ --tea | --no-tea | --coffee ]
.RI [ tool-options ]
.RI [ operands... ]
.PP
.B tea
.RB [ last | list | show
.RI [ id ]
.RB | config | which
.IR tool ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tea
overrides common
.B stdin\(rarrstdout
stream filters with thin wrappers that pass every flag through to the real
binary.  When
.B activated ,
each wrapper copies its
.B stdin
to a temporary file created with
.B mktemp\ --suffix\ .tea
(usually under
.BR /tmp )
and appends an index row to
.B ./logs.csv
before/while running the underlying command.
Stdout of the real command is left untouched so pipelines stay valid
.RI ( "nix eval \&... | jq"
still works).
.PP
Status blurbs go to
.B stderr
only \(one line, e.g.
.BR "[tea] --id 60 /tmp/tmp.XXXXXX.tea | tail -150" ).
.PP
Manual pages for the underlying tools
.RB ( man\ grep ,
.BR man\ sort ,
etc.) are unchanged.
Use
.B man tea
for this behaviour.
.PP
.B tee
and
.B cat
are
.B not
wrapped \(em they do not mutate stdout content (fan-out / identity).
.
.SH WRAPPED TOOLS
Filters that mutate the byte/line stream (selection, rewrite, reorder,
reshape, dump/count).  Canonical list lives in
.IR tools.nix .
.PP
.B Selection / slicing:
grep, egrep, fgrep, head, tail, cut
.PP
.B Rewriting:
sed, awk, gawk, tr, expand, unexpand, fmt, fold, nl, numfmt
.PP
.B Ordering / uniqueness:
sort, uniq, shuf, tac, rev, tsort
.PP
.B Columnar / paste:
paste, join, column, col, colrm
.PP
.B Dump / count:
od, hexdump, hd, wc
.PP
.B Misc text:
pr, ptx, comm
.PP
BusyBox-shaped workflows are covered by wrapping the GNU/util-linux binaries
NixOS puts on PATH (same applet names).
.
.SH ACTIVATION
Activation is evaluated in this order:
.TP
.B --coffee , --no-tea
Force off for this invocation.
.TP
.B --tea
Force on for this invocation.
.TP
.B TEA_OFF=1 / TEA_FORCE=1
Environment overrides (off / on); CLI flags win over these.
.TP
.B manual-only
When true in config, only
.B --tea
(or
.BR TEA_FORCE )
activates.
.TP
.B agentic session
Detected via common agent environment variables
.RI ( CURSOR_AGENT , CLAUDECODE , COPILOT_CLI , AEGIS , HERMES_AGENT , AGENT , ...)
or parent process names. Also
.BR TEA_AGENT=1 .
.TP
.B interactive session
When
.B default-interactive
is true (default): stderr is a TTY, stdin is a pipe, and the parent process
is the user's shell (not POSIX
.BR sh / dash,
nor a terminal emulator's internal
.BR fish\ -c /
.BR sh\ -c
pipeline under
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ,
nor a deeply nested subprocess with
.B SHLVL
≥ 5 under
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ).
.B TEA_USER_PIPE=1
(from the fish hook or set manually) also marks user pipeline stages.
.TP
.B tea which
At the shell prompt,
.B interactive
is usually false (stdin is still the TTY). Use
.BR interactive_if_piped ,
.BR would_activate_if_piped ,
.B --piped ,
or pipe into
.B tea\ which
to preview pipeline activation.
.TP
.B --tea / --coffee
Only work on tea-wrapped binaries from this package (must be ahead of the
real utilities on
.BR PATH ).
The wrapper strips the flag before execing the real tool.
.PP
Non-interactive scripts without agent markers are a no-op: the real binary is
.BR exec 'd
with tea flags stripped and
.B zero
extra stderr.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $TMPDIR/tmp.XXXXXX.tea
Captured stdin for each activated stage
.RI ( mktemp\ --suffix\ .tea ;
typically under
.BR /tmp ).
Ephemeral by design.
.TP
.I ./logs.csv
Index of the last
.I N
stages (default 20). Oldest rows and their
.B .tea
files are deleted when a new
row would exceed
.BR max-log-records .
Columns:
.IR id , timestamp , cwd , tool , command , highlighted , logfile , bytes , exit_code .
The
.B logfile
column stores the absolute
.B .tea
path. The
.B highlighted
column marks the active stage with ⟦\&...⟧ inside a best-effort pipeline
reconstruction.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tea/config.toml
Created on first use. Per-tool keys:
.BR enabled ,
.BR only-in-git-repos ,
.BR only-outside-git-repos ,
.BR update-gitignore ,
.BR default-interactive
.RI ( alias:\ default-intactive ),
.BR manual-only ,
.BR max-log-records ,
.BR quiet .
.TP
.I .gitignore
When
.B update-gitignore
is true and the cwd is inside a git work tree,
.B logs.csv
is appended if missing.
.
.SH OPTIONS (WRAPPERS)
These flags are consumed by tea and
.B not
passed to the underlying tool:
.TP
.B --tea
Enable logging for this invocation.
.TP
.B --no-tea , --coffee
Disable logging for this invocation.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B tea last
Print the path of the most recent logfile (cwd-relative
.IR ./logs.csv ).
.TP
.B tea list
Dump
.I ./logs.csv
to stdout.
.TP
.B tea show [id]
Print logfile contents (default: last id).
.TP
.B tea config
Ensure and print the path to
.IR config.toml .
.TP
.B tea which TOOL
Print whether activation would fire for
.I TOOL
and the effective config (including
.BR systemd_unit ,
.BR invocation_id ,
and
.BR systemd_suppress ,
.BR user_pipeline ,
.BR tea_user_pipe ,
.BR interactive_if_piped ,
.BR would_activate_if_piped ,
and optional
.BR --tea / --coffee / --no-tea / --piped
flags).
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TEA_OFF
If truthy, disable activation unless
.B --tea
is passed (CLI flags win).
.TP
.B TEA_FORCE
If truthy, force activation unless
.B --coffee / --no-tea
is passed.
.TP
.B TEA_QUIET
Suppress the stderr blurb.
.TP
.B TEA_AGENT / TEA_INTERACTIVE
Force agentic / interactive detection.
.TP
.B TEA_USER_PIPE
When truthy, mark the process as a user pipeline stage for interactive
auto-activation. Set by the fish hook shipped in
.IR share/fish/vendor_conf.d/tea-user-pipe.fish
(or manually). Does not bypass
.B --coffee / --no-tea .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Config root (default
.IR ~/.config ).
.PP
Precedence:
.BR --coffee / --no-tea
>
.B --tea
>
.B TEA_OFF
>
.B TEA_FORCE
>
config / auto-detect.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.NF
# CLAUDE.md-style build filter — pre-filter stream lands in /tmp/*.tea
nix build .#nixosConfigurations.void.config.system.build.toplevel --no-link \\
  2>&1 | grep error -C 10 | sort | uniq | tail -100
tea last
tea show

# Force on inside a script
some-cmd | grep --tea pattern

# Force off in an interactive terminal
some-cmd | grep --coffee pattern | head --coffee

# Clean stdout for jq (blurbs already on stderr; coffee for belt-and-braces)
nix eval --json .#foo | jq .
.FI
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR awk (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR tail (1),
.BR tapper (1)
.
.SH NOTES
.B sh-tea
is the project/repo name; the installed CLI and every env var stay
.B tea
for brevity.
.B sh-tea
also ships on PATH as a plain alias for
.B tea
(same binary, same flags, same config) for anyone who typed the full name.
.PP
.B tea
is intentionally weaker than
.BR tapper (1):
tapper is foresight (you hand it the whole pipeline string).
tea is hindsight bolted onto everyday habits \(em no new command name to
remember, and agent instructions that already say
.RB \*(lq grep | sort | tail \*(rq
keep working.
